-- ===== 280: state.instruction_drift — FULL-TEXT comparison against ops/routine_backup.json =====
-- (2026-11-23, interactive session, operator-directed: "fix it".)
-- Project: stock-trading-498512. Apply after bigquery/201 (and bigquery/15, which it joins).
-- Defines exactly two views: state.routine_canonical_full_instruction (new) and
-- state.instruction_drift (redefined). Creates, alters or drops nothing else. Idempotent.
--
-- ============================================================================================
-- WHY — the gap this closes.
-- ============================================================================================
-- state.instruction_drift (bigquery/201, and 183/115/15 before it) compares each routine's newest
-- logged trigger text (state.routine_last_instruction <- ops.run_log.instruction) against
-- ops.routine_catalog.canonical_instruction — which is ONLY the core one-liner ("Read
-- Claude_Task_Plan.md. Perform OPS0 — regular routine."), by a normalized STARTS_WITH PREFIX match.
-- Everything after that first line of the live trigger — the standing operator paragraphs ("Spawn
-- Sonnet ...", "Do not give final response ...", the SEARCH GUARD) — was STRUCTURALLY INVISIBLE: no
-- edit there could ever set `drifted`.
--
-- THE INCIDENT. Owner commit 6c01e91 (2026-11-16) added one sentence to every routine's SEARCH GUARD
-- in ops/routine_backup.json — the FULL canonical instruction per routine, the file OPS0 STEP 3 /
-- Q4 step E diff the live triggers against. It was not rolled out to the 33 live triggers for a week
-- (rolled out by the owner 2026-11-23 08:10-08:18Z), and the nightly check reported "0 drifted"
-- every night of that week while all 33 were out of date. Only OPS0's weekly RemoteTrigger sweep can
-- see a change below the first line, and its scheduled runs could not perform that read.
--
-- THE FIX. A second, full-text comparison is ADDED alongside the unchanged core-line one:
--   * state.routine_canonical_full_instruction — one row per FLEET routine (ops/trigger_ids.json minus
--     `_meta`; never the operator's personal_* triggers) carrying routine_backup.json's FULL
--     instruction verbatim, generated (below) by scripts/gen_routine_lists.py, plus the ONE version
--     that text replaced (superseded_full_instruction; NULL for a routine with no earlier version).
--   * state.instruction_drift compares the logged sample against it — but ONLY when the sample is
--     evidence of the full text (see the classifier below), because most runs do not transcribe it —
--     and raises only a CORROBORATED difference (see CORROBORATION below).
--
-- ============================================================================================
-- THE EVIDENCE CLASSIFIER — why "full_text_evidence" is needed and how it was chosen.
-- ============================================================================================
-- Measured live, ops.run_log with log_ts >= 2026-11-15 (every routine), the logged instruction comes in
-- three shapes:
--   (a) CORE ONLY (~53-84 chars) — most runs. Not evidence about the paragraphs either way.
--   (b) FULL TRANSCRIPTION (~1839-1897 chars) — 13 samples across AR_orc, D2, D2a, D3, M1R, OPS0, OPS1,
--       SL2, SL5. EVERY one, after the dash/whitespace normalization below, is byte-identical to the
--       then-live canonical (today's routine_backup.json text minus 6c01e91's sentence " A Bash curl
--       fetch of a key-less endpoint that the plan pins is not a web tool and stays allowed."). So a
--       full transcription is RELIABLE evidence — the agents copy it verbatim — and a mismatch on one
--       is a real disagreement between the live trigger and the repo.
--   (c) PARTIAL SUMMARY (~141-620 chars) — ends in a placeholder such as "SEARCH GUARD. [...Parallel
--       search guard paragraph per trigger...]", "[Parallel Search guard paragraph]", or "SEARCH GUARD
--       per stored prompt)". Also M5/Q4 samples (529 chars, 2026-10/11) that stop after the "Do not
--       give final response" paragraph. NOT evidence; must never be reported as drift.
-- NORMALIZATION (both sides, full-text half only): the core check's em/en-dash folding and whitespace
-- collapse, PLUS quote folding — ' " ` and the curly quotes are dropped. The canonical carries
-- apostrophes ("run's", "routine's") that an agent must escape inside the sp_routine_start SQL literal;
-- dropping one is the obvious workaround and is a transcription slip, never a trigger difference.
-- Rule: full_text_evidence =
--   (i)  the normalized sample EQUALS the normalized superseded_full_instruction — proof, at any length,
--        that the live trigger still carries the text this canonical replaced; OR
--   (ii) normalized length >= 0.85 x normalized canonical-full length AND the raw sample contains no
--        placeholder (pattern below — kept in LOCKSTEP with scripts/gen_routine_lists.py
--        PLACEHOLDER_PATTERN, pinned by tests/test_gen_routine_lists_full_text.py, and the generator
--        refuses any canonical text that would itself match it).
-- Why (ii) needs both halves:
--   * "no '[' at all" is NOT usable — canonical text itself contains "[Routine paused]".
--   * length alone would pass a ~1800-char sample that elides one short paragraph behind a "[...]";
--     the placeholder test alone would pass a 141-char summary that uses no brackets. Shapes (a) and
--     (c) all fail the length half (largest partial measured: 620 chars vs a >= 1925-char canonical,
--     both normalized).
--   * 0.85 tolerates a full transcription that differs in a sentence or two (exactly the drift we want
--     to see) while staying far above every partial shape measured.
-- Why (i) is needed: (ii) measures length against the NEW canonical, so a verbatim copy of a stale
-- trigger is invisible to it whenever the edit grew the text by more than ~15% (the 2026-11-14 SEARCH
-- GUARD addition grew every canonical ~530 -> ~1936 chars; a trigger left un-rolled-out then would have
-- read "0 drifted" exactly as in the incident). SEED: superseded_full_instruction is the pre-6c01e91
-- text (6c01e91^:ops/routine_backup.json) for all 33.
--
-- CORROBORATION — a full-text difference sets drifted only when it is corroborated: the sample equals
-- the superseded canonical (rule (i)), OR at least two logged samples dated after canonical_full_since
-- carry exactly the same normalized text (n_identical_since >= 2). A real stale trigger is transcribed
-- identically run after run (measured: D2a 11-19 = 11-22; SL5 11-16 = 11-18 = 11-19), a random
-- mis-transcription (a silently dropped paragraph, a one-word slip) is not — and an uncorroborated one
-- would raise a WARNING about a correct trigger, re-raised daily until a monthly/quarterly routine next
-- ran. An uncorroborated difference is published as drift_unproven (full_text_corroborated = FALSE).
-- ACCEPTED LIMITATION: a live trigger that LOST a whole paragraph (shorter than 85%) and is not the
-- superseded text reads as "no evidence", not as drift. The live RemoteTrigger diffs — OPS0 STEP 3's
-- weekly sweep and Q4 step E — remain the only detectors for that shape (scripts/routine_backup.py check
-- validates the backup file offline and never reads a live trigger). Likewise a trigger TWO canonical
-- edits stale falls back to rule (ii): only one superseded version is kept.
-- ACCEPTED LIMITATION: the comparison uses each routine's NEWEST sample, as the core check always has.
-- Evidence therefore exists only on days a run happened to transcribe in full; a core-only run after a
-- full-text mismatch withdraws the evidence (and the alert) until the next full transcription. Using
-- the newest FULL sample instead was rejected: after the owner corrects a live trigger, a stale full
-- sample would keep asserting drift for as long as no later run transcribes in full — an indefinite
-- false alarm about a correct trigger, the class bigquery/183 exists to prevent.
--
-- STALENESS GATE — canonical_full_since. Identical semantics to canonical_since (bigquery/183/201): a
-- sample whose run_date is ON OR BEFORE canonical_full_since cannot be evidence about the CURRENT
-- canonical, so a mismatch on it is published as drift_unproven, never as drifted. The generator
-- PRESERVES a routine's date while its text is unchanged and re-stamps it (America/Denver date — the
-- plane ops.run_log.run_date is keyed in) when the text changes. SEED: 2026-11-23 for all 33, the day
-- the live rollout of 6c01e91 landed; D2 and D2a's newest samples are pre-rollout FULL transcriptions
-- from 2026-11-22 and would otherwise raise a drift alert about triggers that are now correct.
-- Note what this gate does NOT hide: once routine_backup.json changes, the date moves to that day, and
-- every full transcription logged AFTER it that still carries the old text sets drifted — that is
-- exactly the un-rolled-out-week this file exists to catch.
--
-- SYNTHETIC CONTROLS (run 2026-11-23 against live data as plain SELECTs, before any apply):
--   current canonical_full_since (2026-11-23): drifted = FALSE for all 33 routines; D2/D2a report
--     full_text_evidence = TRUE, full_text_mismatch = TRUE, drift_unproven = TRUE (pre-rollout sample).
--   canonical_full_since forced to 2026-11-15: D2 and D2a (newest sample = pre-rollout full text,
--     2026-11-22) -> drifted = TRUE; core-only samples (D1, OPS0, W1, ...) and the 529-char M5/Q4
--     partial samples -> drifted = FALSE, full_text_evidence = FALSE.
--   every measured placeholder sample (D2 11-17/11-18, D2a 11-18, D3 11-16) -> full_text_evidence = FALSE.
--   every 2026-11-15+ full transcription compared to its own then-live text -> full_text_mismatch = FALSE.
--   a full transcription with one sentence added or removed -> full_text_mismatch = TRUE.
--   with the superseded column + corroboration (re-run 2026-11-23): normal -> drifted FALSE for all 33,
--     D2/D2a matches_superseded_full = TRUE but drift_unproven (pre-rollout); forced -> D2/D2a drifted =
--     TRUE via matches_superseded_full. Synthetic samples dated after canonical_full_since: the exact
--     superseded text -> drifted; canonical with apostrophes dropped -> no mismatch; one "Spawn Sonnet"
--     paragraph silently dropped, "run's own" -> "run own", or one reworded sentence, each seen ONCE ->
--     full_text_mismatch but drift_unproven (uncorroborated); the same paragraph-drop seen TWICE ->
--     drifted; a placeholder summary -> no evidence; the exact canonical -> no mismatch.
--
-- HOW THE CANONICAL STAYS IN SYNC. The region below is GENERATED from ops/routine_backup.json by
-- `python scripts/gen_routine_lists.py --write`; CI's `gen_routine_lists.py --check` FAILS when it is
-- stale, so a routine_backup.json edit cannot merge without regenerating this file.
-- LANDING — do NOT leave the live apply to D3's self-heal. live-sql-parity (daily) + D3's CI-FINDINGS
-- self-heal (skips same-day findings, runs Sun-Thu) would leave the live canonical view stale for
-- ~2-5 days, and in that window (1) triggers rolled out to the NEW text are compared with the OLD
-- canonical (false drift once corroborated), or (2) un-rolled-out triggers still match the old canonical
-- (the original silent miss). On the FIRST apply, the self-heal also has no object order: re-creating
-- state.instruction_drift before state.routine_canonical_full_instruction exists fails, and the 7-day
-- self-heal latch can then keep the check dark for a week. So, in this order:
--   1. gen_routine_lists.py --write;  2. gen_dbt_port.py state.routine_canonical_full_instruction;
--   3. after merge, apply this file live yourself, statement (1) first, then (2);
--   4. only then roll the changed instructions out to the live triggers;
--   5. if the rollout landed on a later day than step 1, `gen_routine_lists.py --write --full-since
--      <rollout date>` and land + apply that too, so a routine whose last run transcribed the
--      pre-rollout text (a monthly/quarterly one may not run again for weeks) is not raised as drift.
-- `gen_routine_lists.py` prints these steps whenever this region is stale or rewritten.
-- The raise site (ops.sp_sq_cadence_check, bigquery/241) reads only drifted / unknown_routine / routine
-- / live_instruction / canonical_instruction, all kept, so it needs no edit.
-- TRIAGE NOTE: the bigquery/241 alert payload carries live_instruction (the full logged text) and
-- canonical_instruction (the CORE LINE only). A drifted row whose live_instruction STARTS WITH its
-- canonical_instruction is a FULL-TEXT drift, not a false alarm: read this view's full_text_mismatch /
-- matches_superseded_full / full_text_corroborated and diff live_instruction against
-- state.routine_canonical_full_instruction.canonical_full_instruction for that routine.
--
-- SUPERSEDES `state.instruction_drift` in bigquery/201_instruction_drift_dash_normalize.sql (which
-- superseded 183, 115 and 15). Every column 201 published is kept, in order; new columns are appended
-- after checked_at. drifted / drift_unproven gain the full-text half: drifted is 201's value OR a
-- corroborated, post-canonical_full_since full-text difference; drift_unproven is "a mismatch reported
-- but not raised" (201: a core mismatch on a stale sample; now also a stale or uncorroborated full-text
-- mismatch) and is FALSE whenever drifted is TRUE — so it can read FALSE where 201 read TRUE, when the
-- full-text half proves drift. No consumer reads drift_unproven (checked 2026-11-23).

-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- (1) state.routine_canonical_full_instruction — FULL canonical trigger text per fleet routine.
-- Do NOT hand-edit the marked region — edit ops/routine_backup.json and re-run
-- `python scripts/gen_routine_lists.py --write`. Raw triple-quoted literals: the text carries
-- apostrophes and newlines; the generator refuses a text containing ''' or a backslash.
-- superseded_full_instruction = the full text this routine's canonical replaced (preserved while the
-- canonical is unchanged; NULL when there is none).
-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
